 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#what-actually-works-bringing-aeo-analysis-together"&gt;What Actually Works: Bringing AEO Analysis Together&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;What is AEO analysis and how is it different from SEO?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AEO analysis measures how a brand is represented inside AI-generated answers. That includes whether the brand is mentioned by name, cited as a source, characterized favorably, or left out entirely for relevant prompts.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://blog.hubspot.com/hs-fs/hubfs/Frank%20H/aeo-analysis-2-20261021-9784428.webp?width=650&amp;amp;height=550&amp;amp;name=aeo-analysis-2-20261021-9784428.webp" width="650" height="550" alt="Comparison chart showing SEO analysis vs AEO analysis metrics and goals" style="margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block; width: 650px; height: auto; max-width: 100%;"&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Traditional SEO analysis is built around the search engine results page — where a domain ranks for a given keyword, how many clicks and impressions it earns, and how that position shifts over time. AEO analysis works differently because answer engines do not return a ranked list of links. They return synthesized answers built from a handful of sources. That means there is no ranking position to track, only presence, framing, and share of voice.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;What is an answer engine?&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;An answer engine is a platform like ChatGPT, Gemini, or Perplexity that responds to a query with a direct, synthesized answer. Traditional search engines return a list of links and leave the user to decide which source to trust. An answer engine reads across multiple sources, pulls out the relevant information, and presents a single response, often citing the sources it drew from along the way.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The distinction matters for brands because answer engines are increasingly where buyers start their research. A brand that appears prominently and favorably in those answers has an advantage that does not show up in traditional SEO metrics. A brand that is absent or mischaracterized has a visibility problem that keyword rankings alone cannot diagnose.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Does “Google Answer Box Optimization” still matter?&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Google Answer Box Optimization still matters. Structuring content to win the featured snippet at the top of a traditional Google search results page remains best practice. Featured snippets appear less frequently than they once did, but the structural habits that helped win them — clear definitions, concise steps, and well-formatted lists — are the same habits that support a strong &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/aeo-b2b-strategy"&gt;AEO strategy&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;What a Good AEO Analysis Should Measure&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Before comparing AEO analysis tools (not to be mistaken with &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/seo-analysis-tools"&gt;SEO analysis tools&lt;/a&gt;), it helps to establish what marketers should track.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Brand Visibility Across Answer Engines&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Brand visibility is an AEO metric that counts how often AI platforms mention or cite a brand for specified prompts. It is a foundational metric in AEO analysis, though it has drawn criticism as a vanity metric since visibility does not always translate directly to clicks or conversions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;My perspective is that brand visibility is the prerequisite for clicks and conversions, not a substitute for them. Without visibility, a brand cannot get the click — and without the click, there is no conversion. That makes it essential to track, even if it sits earlier in the funnel than most marketers are used to measuring.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Research shows the value of visibility even when websites are not getting clicked. Responsive’s report, &lt;a href="https://www.responsive.io/resources/whitepapers/inside-the-b2b-buyers-mind-2025"&gt;Inside the Buyer’s Mind&lt;/a&gt;, found that nearly as many potential buyers start searching with generative AI chatbots (32%) as with traditional web search (33%). In the Technology and Software industry, that number climbs significantly — buyers use generative AI for as many as 56% of searches.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;HubSpot’s research shows a similar pattern of audience-specific AI adoption. According to &lt;a href="https://offers.hubspot.com/ai-marketing"&gt;HubSpot’s 2025 AI Trends for Marketers&lt;/a&gt; report, nearly 31% of Gen Zers default to chatbots or AI to find information online. Both findings point to the same conclusion: different audiences engage with generative AI at very different rates, which means brand visibility in AI answers matters more for some markets than others.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Pro tip:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span&gt; Knowing who’s in the audience and how they search helps determine how heavily to prioritize AEO analysis, though every brand stands to benefit from it.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;This audience-level split shows up in HunSpot’s research on the CRM buying journey too.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;HubSpot’s Q1 2026 Competitive NPS Survey of 3,063 CRM...
